# =============================================================================
# .gitignore  —  Agri-Finance Analytics
#
# Git tracks every file in this folder unless a pattern here tells it not to.
# Read this file before the first commit. Once a large file is in Git history
# it stays there even after you delete it, and removing it means rewriting
# history — which is painful and easy to get wrong.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. DATA
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw data is excluded for three separate reasons, any one of which is enough:
#   (a) Size. Git stores a full copy of every version of every file. A 200 MB
#       CSV that changes three times is 600 MB of repository, forever.
#   (b) Licence. Some public datasets permit reuse but not redistribution.
#       Linking to the source in SOURCES.md is always safe; re-hosting is not.
#   (c) Reproducibility is the point. If someone must run the ingestion script
#       to get the data, then the ingestion script is proven to work. If the
#       data is committed, a broken script can sit unnoticed for months.
#
# The pattern excludes the CONTENTS of the folder, not the folder itself,
# which is why the .gitkeep negation below works.
data/raw/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep

data/interim/*
!data/interim/.gitkeep

data/clean/*
!data/clean/.gitkeep


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. PYTHON
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# __pycache__ holds compiled bytecode. It is generated automatically, it is
# machine-specific, and committing it creates merge conflicts in files no
# human ever reads.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# The virtual environment. This can be thousands of files and hundreds of MB,
# and it is rebuildable from requirements.txt in one command. Committing it
# is the single most common beginner mistake in a Python repo.
.venv/
venv/
env/
*.egg-info/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. NOTEBOOKS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jupyter's autosave directory. Not the notebooks themselves — those ARE
# committed, because the profiling notebook is a deliverable of Phase 2.
.ipynb_checkpoints/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. SECRETS AND LOCAL CONFIG
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# config.ini holds the database connection for THIS machine. It is excluded
# for two reasons: it may contain a password, and even without one the server
# name is specific to your laptop and would break on anyone else's.
#
# config.example.ini IS committed. It has the same structure with the values
# blanked out, so a stranger cloning the repo knows what to fill in.
config.ini
.env


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. OFFICE AND OS CLUTTER
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Word and Excel create hidden lock files starting with ~$ while a file is
# open. They are meaningless outside your session. This matters here because
# the phase documents are .docx files sitting inside the repo.
~$*

# Windows and OneDrive folder metadata. desktop.ini in particular is created
# by OneDrive in synced folders and would otherwise be committed by accident.
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# Editor settings. Personal preference, not project configuration.
.vscode/
.idea/

# The Claude desktop app drops copies of files it delivers into the chat here.
# They are duplicates of files that already live in the repo (the phase
# documents), so committing them would track the same content twice and the
# two copies would drift. Added 19 September 2026 after the folder appeared
# during Phase 0.
Claude outputs/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. PHASE DOCUMENTS  —  a decision, not a default
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The phase write-ups in phases/ are deliberately NOT published.
#
# The reasoning, recorded because the opposite choice is also defensible:
# these documents are only valuable if they record the work honestly, wrong
# turns included. A document written knowing strangers will read it gets
# tidied, and a tidied log is worthless as a record of how the work actually
# went. Keeping them private protects the candour.
#
# The public version is not abandoned — at Phase 7 a "how this was built"
# document is written in markdown, drawn from these nine logs and edited for
# an audience. That gets the differentiator without the self-censorship.
#
# Decided 19 September 2026. If this is ever reversed, note that the logs
# were written under the assumption of privacy.
#
# NOTE the leading slash. A bare "phases/" matches a directory of that name at
# ANY depth, which would also have swallowed docs/phases/. The leading slash
# anchors the pattern to the repository root, so it means this one folder and
# nothing else. Caught by a check, not by reading the line back.
/phases/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. POWER BI  —  a decision, not a default
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .pbix files are NOT ignored. The dashboard is part of the portfolio and a
# reviewer should be able to open it.
#
# But there is a trap, and it is worth understanding before Phase 6:
# a .pbix built with Import mode embeds a compressed copy of the data inside
# the file. Commit that and the raw data enters the repo through the back
# door — defeating section 1 entirely, and possibly breaching a licence.
#
# The mitigation is to connect Power BI to SQL Server with DirectQuery or a
# Live connection, so the .pbix holds only the model and the visuals.
# Check the file size before committing. If it is more than a few MB, the
# data is inside it and you need to look at the connection mode again.
#
# If it turns out Import mode is unavoidable, uncomment the next line and
# say so in the README — an excluded dashboard with a stated reason is
# honest; an accidentally committed 80 MB file is not.
# powerbi/*.pbix
